# UI Style Contract

The DS Dashboard has its own visual identity — Dark SaaS, professional, decoupled from Karmap/Iter. Every visual decision flows from centralized tokens in `index.css` through `tailwind.config.ts` into components. This contract ensures that chain is never bypassed.

## Token-first styling

All colors, surfaces, borders, shadows, and motion values **MUST** come from the token system. Never use raw values.

### DO

```tsx
<div className="bg-surface-1 text-foreground border-border shadow-md" />
<Card variant="elevated" />
<StatusAlert variant="error" title="Failed to sync" />
```

### DON'T

```tsx
<div className="bg-[#161618] text-[#fafafa] border-[#3a3a3f]" />
<div className="bg-gray-900 text-gray-100 border-gray-700" />
<div className="rounded-md border border-red-500/30 bg-red-500/10 p-3 text-red-700">Error</div>
```

## Prohibited color patterns

The following raw Tailwind color classes are **FORBIDDEN** in `features/**`:

```
bg-red-*    bg-emerald-*    bg-amber-*    bg-green-*    bg-yellow-*
text-red-*  text-emerald-*  text-amber-*  text-green-*  text-yellow-*
border-red-* border-emerald-* border-amber-* border-green-* border-yellow-*
```

**Use instead:** `status-error`, `status-success`, `status-warning` tokens — or wrap in `<StatusAlert>` / `<Badge>` with semantic variants.

**Exception:** Dynamic user-provided colors rendered with `style={{ backgroundColor: swatch }}` are allowed when the color is data, not design. Document with a comment.

## Inline styles

### Forbidden

Structural styling (layout, spacing, colors, borders) via `style={{}}`.

```tsx
// ✗ Never
<div style={{ display: 'flex', gap: '16px', backgroundColor: '#1a1a1a' }}>
```

### Allowed (with justification)

Dynamic values that cannot be expressed as Tailwind classes:

```tsx
// ✓ User-provided color swatch
<div style={{ backgroundColor: tokenHexValue }} />

// ✓ Calculated dimension
<div style={{ width: `${percentage}%` }} />

// ✓ Tree depth indentation
<div style={{ paddingLeft: `${depth * 14 + 8}px` }} />
```

## Portals and overlays

- **MUST** use `<Modal>` from `ui/overlay/modal.tsx` for all modal dialogs.
- **MUST NOT** use `createPortal` outside of `ui/overlay/`.
- `Modal` handles scroll lock, escape key, backdrop, and z-index stacking.

### DO

```tsx
import { Modal, ModalContent } from "@/components/ui/overlay/modal";

<Modal open={isOpen} onClose={() => setIsOpen(false)}>
  <ModalContent size="lg">...</ModalContent>
</Modal>
```

### DON'T

```tsx
import { createPortal } from "react-dom";

// Manual portal with isMounted guard, scroll lock, escape listener…
{isMounted && createPortal(<div className="fixed inset-0 z-[1000]">...</div>, document.body)}
```

## Typography

| Element | Class | Hierarchy Role | Rationale |
|---------|-------|----------------|-----------|
| Page/section headings | `font-titles` | Title hierarchy | Strong visual structure for primary and secondary headings |
| UI text, labels, body | `font-body` | Body hierarchy | Consistent readability for dense interface content |
| Token paths, code | `font-mono` | Technical/code hierarchy | Monospace rhythm for paths, values, and code-like data |

- **MUST** use semantic heading levels (`<h1>` page, `<h2>` section, `<h3>` subsection).
- **SHOULD** apply title hierarchy to `<h1>` and `<h2>` via `PageHeader` and `SectionHeader` composites.

## Dark mode

The app runs in permanent dark mode (`class="dark"` on `<html>`).

- **MUST NOT** write light-mode-specific styles.
- **MUST NOT** use `dark:` prefix — it is redundant and creates confusion about intent.
- If dark mode toggle is added in the future, the token layer (`index.css`) handles the switch — components never change.

## Motion

| Speed | Tailwind class | Use case |
|-------|---------------|----------|
| Fast (150ms) | `duration-fast` | Hover states, micro-interactions |
| Base (250ms) | `duration-base` | Panel transitions, content reveals |
| Slow (400ms) | `duration-slow` | Page-level transitions, modals |

- **SHOULD** use these duration tokens instead of arbitrary values.
- **MUST** respect `prefers-reduced-motion` (enforced globally in `index.css`).
- Prefer `ease-out` for entrances, `ease-in-out` for state changes.

## Enforcement

- Violations in new code **MUST** be fixed.
- Existing violations: fix when touching the file (boy scout rule).
- When unsure whether a pattern is allowed, prefer the token system — it exists to absorb these decisions centrally.

### Verification

Use the style contract and related UI governance rules when reviewing dashboard changes.
